.venv/
__pycache__/

# Written by the MCP framework itself at runtime (the FTS index). Copying a host-built one
# in would ship an index whose cache key does not match this image's HEAD, so it would be
# discarded and rebuilt on the first request anyway.
_meta/.cache/

# Generated at deploy time by the pages workflow; never needed at runtime.
site/

# THE REASON THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, and why it is worth having before there is a
# Dockerfile to pair it with.
#
# This corpus is snapshot_policy: hash-only -- `_meta/snapshots/*.pdf` is gitignored and
# never committed (789 sources, 1.2 GB). So a build from a shallow clone, which is what the
# platform's Dockerfiles instruct, cannot see the PDFs and needs no rule to exclude them.
#
# A build from THIS WORKING TREE can, and would: `COPY . .` does not consult .gitignore. It
# reads the build context, and the context here holds the full 1.2 GB. That is the specific
# accident this file prevents -- the one an ingest run leaves loaded and ready.
#
# The committed .txt extractions go too, and unlike the PDFs that needs justifying rather
# than assuming. They are the provenance evidence corpus-verify-provenance checks the
# `## Full text` body against, and they are what source_sha256 hashes. Verified for THIS
# corpus, not inherited from a sibling: the only readers under src/ are build_series.py and
# ingest_kpm.py, both offline; src/citation_schemes.py -- the one plugin the server loads,
# per plugins.citation_module -- does not reference snapshots at all; and there is no
# reference to snapshots anywhere under corpus_toolkit/mcp/.
#
# federal-reference is the counter-example that makes the check mandatory rather than
# ceremonial: there, src/citation_schemes.py reads a snapshot .txt AT IMPORT, so excluding
# this directory raised FileNotFoundError before the server answered a single request. The
# rule is not "snapshots are never runtime" -- it is "confirm which files the citation
# module opens, per corpus."
#
# Consequence, stated so it is not a surprise: `corpus-verify-provenance` cannot run INSIDE
# the container. It is a CI gate and belongs there; run it against a checkout, not an image.
_meta/snapshots/

# NOTE: .git is deliberately NOT ignored -- it is a RUNTIME dependency. The FTS cache key is
# `git rev-parse HEAD` plus a hash of `git status --porcelain`, and corpus_overview() shells
# out to `git log -1`. Without it repo_state() collapses to a constant, so content changes
# are never picked up for the life of the process and corpus_overview reports an empty
# commit -- silently, in both cases. Build from a depth-1 clone to keep it small.
#
# Cheap here, unlike next door: this corpus commits no PDFs, so its blobs are markdown and
# text. executive-regulatory-frameworks commits 218 MB of PDFs and therefore carries them in
# /repo/.git even though this same rule excludes them from the working tree -- a cost that
# excluding _meta/snapshots/ does not touch. Nothing to inherit here; just do not add PDFs
# to the repo later and assume this file still covers them.
